    "context": "What counts as an AI bill here is a mechanical answer, not an editorial one. These pages track legislation from LegiScan matching artificial intelligence related keywords and subject codes, refreshed daily. That gives a consistent, auditable rule, and it also means the collection inherits the limits of keyword matching.\n\nExpect the enacted counts to differ from other trackers, sometimes substantially. Inclusion criteria vary. Some count only bills naming artificial intelligence in the title. Others sweep in broader technology policy. A lower number here is not a claim that a state did less.\n\nThe most important thing this map does not show is regulation that already exists. A state with zero tracked bills is not a state with no AI regulation. Consumer protection and deceptive trade practice statutes reach AI marketing claims and automated decisions everywhere. Privacy, breach notification and biometric laws reach how models are trained and how personal data is handled. Deepfake, publicity rights and child protection statutes reach synthetic media directly. Almost none of them mention artificial intelligence, and none of them need to.\n\nRead a zero carefully for two more reasons. Some legislatures meet only in odd numbered years, so an even year shows nothing. Some jurisdictions, Puerto Rico among them, are under covered by the underlying data. And the tracker cuts both ways. It misses real AI bills and it catches false positives, resolutions and unrelated statutes that happen to share vocabulary with AI policy. Where that has happened, the state pages say so by name.",
    "reading": {
        "body": "This dataset tracks 3,277 AI related bills across all 50 states, the District of Columbia, Puerto Rico, and the U.S. Congress, with Congress itself accounting for 688 bills, more than any individual state, followed by New York at 299, New Jersey at 176, California at 175, and Illinois at 168.\n\nThe range between states is enormous. New York's 299 tracked bills sit against zero recorded bills in Montana, Nevada, and Puerto Rico, and just four in Wyoming, a spread that reflects both real differences in legislative activity and how broadly or narrowly each state's legislature generates bills that touch AI, privacy, or automated decision making even in passing.\n\nCongress's 688 bills alone account for roughly a fifth of the total 3,277, and combined with New York, New Jersey, California, and Illinois, the four largest state totals, these five jurisdictions account for close to half of all bills tracked here, while the remaining 47 states and territories share the other half among far smaller individual counts.\n\nThe data does not indicate how many of these 3,277 bills have passed into law, what fraction are still pending, or how many were withdrawn, so the total legislative activity recorded here does not represent the current legal landscape, only the volume of bills introduced.",
